---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const conflictCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Bargaining theory of war', note: 'War is costly — so rational sides should always find a deal better than fighting. Fearon (1995): war happens through (a) private information (each inflates their strength), (b) commitment problems (I can\'t trust you to keep tomorrow\'s deal), (c) indivisible stakes. The field\'s foundational modern result.' },
    { math: '2 · Civil war economics', note: 'Greed vs grievance (Collier): loot-able resources and low income predict civil war better than oppression per se — poverty is the strongest single risk factor. Contested, refined, still the reference frame.' },
    { math: '3 · Peacekeeping works', note: 'Fortna (2008) and the aggregated evidence: peacekeeping deployments cut risk of war resuming roughly in half. The "does the UN work?" debate got an empirical answer for its most-mocked activity.' },
    { math: '4 · Peacebuilding is harder than peacemaking', note: 'Ending fighting (negative peace) ≠ building institutions that resolve disputes without violence (positive peace — Galtung\'s distinction). Power-sharing, transitional justice, DDR (disarm-demobilise-reintegrate): the toolkit, with mixed records honestly reported.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/advanced/conflict-and-peace-studies">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Conflict studies asks <strong>why organised violence happens and how it stops</strong>. Its
    modern core is the bargaining insight — war is a costly failure to make a deal — which
    reframes everything: causes of war are causes of bargaining failure, and peace is the
    engineering of information, commitment and divisibility.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bargaining framework, civil-war risk factors, what peacekeeping does, peacebuilding's honest record" widget="equationStep" config={conflictCfg} />

  <h2>Findings that travel</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Democratic peace is real but limited:</strong> established democracies rarely fight each other; young democracies fight MORE than autocracies (democratisation is dangerous — the transition, not the end-state).</li>
    <li><strong>Power transition, not parity, is the danger zone:</strong> wars cluster when a rising power approaches the ruling one (WWI framing, Thucydides' trap) — the finding behind current great-power anxiety.</li>
    <li><strong>Conflict data improved everything:</strong> the Correlates of War and UCDP datasets turned "why war?" from essay question into measurable research — the statistics ladder's methods at their most consequential.</li>
    <li><strong>Terrorism is communication (Crenshaw):</strong> violence by the weak, aimed at audiences — explaining the target logic (symbolism, media) better than pure-irrationality models. Counter-terrorism built on the wrong model fails.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fearon, "Rationalist Explanations for War" (1995); Collier & Hoeffler; Fortna, Does Peacekeeping Work? (2008); Galtung (1969).</p>
</TopicPage>
